# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx trace collect console dir
vx trace print file ...
vx trace summary file ...
vx trace diff a b
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx trace` reads files in the format of trace(6).
A <file> may be a directory, which stands for the `.trace` files in it, sorted by name.
It runs from any directory.

: `collect` <console> <dir>
  Reads the text of a boot's console, finds the `td-trace` lines that a trace session exported through the debuglog, and rebuilds the files they carry as `<dir>/<name>.trace`.
  It removes <dir> first.
  The lines are in trace(6).
  It fails, naming the piece, if one is missing (the console drops lines when it falls behind), if a file has no end line, or if a rebuilt file doesn't read as a trace; and it fails if the console carries no traces.
  A line sent twice is taken once.
: `print` <file> ...
  For each file a line `# <path>: process <id>, <n> records, <n> dropped`, then every record, merged by time, as time since the recording began, a tab, `thread <tid>`, a tab, and the record: `zone <name> <duration>`, `mark <label>`, `flow <name> id <id>`, `counter <name> = <value>`, or `kind 0x<kind> a <a> b <b>` for any other kind.
  Times are shown with a unit.
: `summary` <file> ...
  For each file a table of zones by name: count, total, median (p50), 99th percentile (p99) and maximum of their durations.
  A second table follows when the file has flows: each flow's count, the step counts it was seen with, and its median, p99 and maximum span, from its first record to its last.
  If records were dropped it says how many.
  When a named directory holds `kernel.trace`, the directory is also read as one timeline of a recording on croi: a table of each call's commonest path through client, kernel and server, with the median time to each step.
: `diff` <a> <b>
  A table of every zone name in either file with the p50 and p99 of each, and the change of p99 from <a> to <b> as a percentage.
  A name absent from a file counts as having no zones there.

The tables are in Markdown.

